<p>Thanks for subscribing. AdConnect started in Nairobi as a real-estate digital marketing agency, and today we help businesses across East Africa with the wider digital and technology work behind growth. Here is what we offer:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Digital marketing</strong>: social ads, SEO and content, email automation, reporting</li>
<li><strong>Web development</strong>: business sites, online shops, portals, M-Pesa integration</li>
<li><strong>Software development</strong>: custom systems, mobile apps, automation, testing</li>
<li><strong>Software design</strong>: UI/UX, architecture, technical specs, prototypes</li>
<li><strong>Database management</strong>: design, migration, tuning, backups, reporting</li>
<li><strong>Consulting</strong>: strategy, code and architecture reviews, technical audits</li>
<li><strong>Data analytics and training</strong>: statistics, dashboards, surveys, and skills training</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What to expect:</strong> about one useful email every 3 days, each with a practical tip you can use the same day. You can unsubscribe at any time.</p>
<p>If you already have something in mind, <a href="/book.html">book a free session</a> or <a href="/proposal.html">submit a project proposal</a> and we will get back to you.</p>','See our services','/our-services.html','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Whether you advertise a flat in Kilimani or plots in Kitengela, most property ads that struggle are missing the same basics. Check yours against this list:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Location first.</strong> Put the neighbourhood in the headline. People search by area before anything else.</li>
<li><strong>A price or a range.</strong> Hiding the price tends to attract browsers rather than buyers.</li>
<li><strong>One standout feature.</strong> Choose a single hook, such as a borehole, a short walk to the main road, or a payment plan.</li>
<li><strong>A strong first image.</strong> Use bright, level, uncluttered photos. The first one decides whether people stop scrolling.</li>
<li><strong>One clear action.</strong> Pick either "Call", "WhatsApp" or "Book a viewing". Several competing buttons dilute results.</li>
</ol>
<p>Then give every ad its own tracking link or phone number so you can see which listing actually produces enquiries.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: an ad is a short conversation. Answer where, how much, why this one, and what to do next.</blockquote>','Plan your property ads','/book.html?category=digital-marketing','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Most visitors to Kenyan business websites arrive on a phone, often on a patchy connection. If a page takes more than a few seconds to show something useful, many people simply leave.</p>
<p>Start by testing your homepage with <strong>Google PageSpeed Insights</strong> (it is free) and look at the mobile score. Then work through these fixes, roughly in order of impact:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Compress images.</strong> Photos straight from a phone camera can be several megabytes. Resize them to the size they are displayed and use WebP or well-compressed JPEG.</li>
<li><strong>Lazy-load below-the-fold images</strong> so they only download when someone scrolls.</li>
<li><strong>Remove what you do not use.</strong> Old plugins, extra fonts and unused scripts add weight.</li>
<li><strong>Turn on caching and compression</strong> at your host.</li>
<li><strong>Use decent hosting</strong>, ideally close to your audience or behind a CDN.</li>
</ol>
<p>Re-test after each change so you know what helped.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: images are usually the biggest quick win.</blockquote>','Get a speed check','/book.html?category=web-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Followers, impressions and likes are easy to count, but they rarely tell you whether money is well spent. Three numbers do a much better job:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cost per lead (CPL)</strong> = total ad spend / number of enquiries. Example: KES 20,000 spent and 40 enquiries gives a CPL of KES 500.</li>
<li><strong>Lead-to-customer rate</strong> = customers / enquiries. If 4 of those 40 enquiries buy, your rate is 10%.</li>
<li><strong>Cost per customer</strong> = ad spend / customers. Here that is KES 5,000, which you can compare with what a customer is worth to you.</li>
</ul>
<p>Those figures are illustrative, but the method is general. Record the numbers weekly in a simple sheet, split by channel (Facebook, Google, referrals), and you will quickly see where to spend more and where to stop.</p>
<p>One caution: small samples bounce around. Judge trends over several weeks, not a single day.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: measure what happens after the click, not just the click.</blockquote>','Talk to a data analyst','/book.html?category=data-analytics','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Many people hesitate to hire an agency because they are unsure what the process looks like. Here is ours:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Free session.</strong> We listen to your goals, constraints and budget range. No pressure and no jargon.</li>
<li><strong>Written scope.</strong> You receive a clear summary of what will be delivered, in what order and at what price in KES.</li>
<li><strong>Milestones.</strong> Work is split into stages, so you see progress early and can adjust.</li>
<li><strong>Regular updates.</strong> You know what is done, what is next and what we need from you.</li>
<li><strong>Review and handover.</strong> We test the work with you, document it and hand over accounts and access that belong to you.</li>
</ol>
<p>We are a small team, so you speak directly with the people doing the work: Misgina on digital marketing and Michael on software, databases and data.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: you should always know the scope, the price and the next step.</blockquote>
<p>Ready to talk? You can also browse everything we do on our services page.</p>','Browse our services','/our-services.html','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Losing customer records, orders or accounting data can stop a small business for days. The <strong>3-2-1 rule</strong> is a simple, widely used way to avoid that:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>3 copies</strong> of your data: the live one plus two backups.</li>
<li><strong>2 different storage types</strong>, for example a server disk and cloud storage.</li>
<li><strong>1 copy off-site</strong>, so a fire, theft or a hosting failure cannot take everything.</li>
</ul>
<p>Three habits make the rule actually work:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Automate it.</strong> Backups that depend on someone remembering will eventually be skipped.</li>
<li><strong>Test a restore</strong> at least once a quarter. A backup you have never restored is only a hope.</li>
<li><strong>Encrypt and restrict access</strong> to backup files, because they contain the same sensitive data as the live system.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: a backup is only real once you have successfully restored from it.</blockquote>','Review my backups','/book.html?category=database-management','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Consistency beats brilliance on social media, and consistency needs a plan. Try this lightweight method:</p>
<h3>1. Choose three content pillars</h3>
<p>For example: <strong>educate</strong> (tips and how-tos), <strong>prove</strong> (your work, reviews with permission, behind the scenes) and <strong>offer</strong> (what you sell and how to buy).</p>
<h3>2. Set a rhythm you can sustain</h3>
<p>Three posts a week for a month is better than daily posts for ten days. Assign a pillar to each slot, for example Monday educate, Wednesday prove, Friday offer.</p>
<h3>3. Batch and repurpose</h3>
<p>Write and design a week of posts in one sitting. One idea can become a post, a short video, a WhatsApp Status and an email paragraph.</p>
<h3>4. Review monthly</h3>
<p>Note which posts earned replies, saves or clicks, and make more of those.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: a simple calendar you follow beats a perfect one you abandon.</blockquote>','Request a marketing plan','/proposal.html?category=digital-marketing','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Good interface design is mostly about clarity. Here is a quick test you can run this afternoon:</p>
<ol>
<li>Show a screen, such as your homepage or an app''s main page, to someone who has never seen it.</li>
<li>After <strong>five seconds</strong>, hide it.</li>
<li>Ask: "What is this for? What would you do next?"</li>
</ol>
<p>If they cannot answer, the screen is asking people to work too hard. Common fixes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Make the hierarchy obvious.</strong> One main heading, one primary button, and less competing noise.</li>
<li><strong>Design for the phone first</strong>, then scale up to the desktop.</li>
<li><strong>Check contrast.</strong> Light grey text on white is hard to read outdoors.</li>
<li><strong>Use plain words</strong> on buttons: "Get a quote" beats "Submit".</li>
</ul>
<p>Sketching in Figma before any code is written makes these problems cheap to fix.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: if a stranger cannot explain your screen in five seconds, simplify it.</blockquote>','Talk to a UI/UX designer','/book.html?category=software-design','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Choosing a developer or agency is a big decision, and a few good questions go a long way:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Who will actually do the work?</strong> Ask to speak to that person.</li>
<li><strong>What exactly is included?</strong> Get scope, deliverables and exclusions in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Who owns the code, designs, domain and accounts</strong> when the project ends?</li>
<li><strong>How will we communicate</strong>, and how often will you show progress?</li>
<li><strong>What happens after launch?</strong> Ask about fixes, hosting and maintenance costs.</li>
<li><strong>Can I see similar work</strong>, and speak to a past client if they agree?</li>
<li><strong>What could go wrong</strong>, and how would you handle it?</li>
</ol>
<p>Pay attention to how clearly the answers are given. A good partner will explain trade-offs honestly, including when something is not needed.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: clear ownership, scope and support terms matter more than the lowest quote.</blockquote>','Book a consulting session','/book.html?category=consulting','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Custom software is not always the answer. Use these questions to decide:</p>
<h3>Buy or subscribe when</h3>
<ul>
<li>Your process is common (accounting, email, basic CRM).</li>
<li>A tool already covers about 80% of your needs.</li>
<li>You need to start this month.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Build when</h3>
<ul>
<li>Your process is your competitive advantage and no tool fits it.</li>
<li>You are stitching together several tools with manual copy-and-paste.</li>
<li>Per-user subscription fees will grow faster than the cost of owning your own system.</li>
<li>You need local integrations such as M-Pesa, specific reports or offline use.</li>
</ul>
<p>A good middle path is to buy the standard parts and build only the missing piece, such as a small integration or automation, rather than a full system.</p>
<p>Whatever you choose, list the three tasks that waste the most time today. They show where software will pay off first.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: build what is unique to you, and buy what is common.</blockquote>','Describe your project','/proposal.html?category=software-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>M-Pesa is how many Kenyan customers prefer to pay, so a smooth checkout can make a real difference. Typically, businesses integrate through Safaricom''s Daraja API, often with the STK Push prompt that asks the customer to enter their PIN. Plan for these points:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Use the sandbox first.</strong> Test every scenario before real money moves.</li>
<li><strong>Confirm payments server-side.</strong> Never mark an order as paid just because the customer''s browser says so. Wait for the callback from Safaricom.</li>
<li><strong>Handle failures kindly.</strong> Customers cancel, run out of balance or time out. Offer a clear "try again" option.</li>
<li><strong>Make callbacks safe.</strong> Use HTTPS, verify the data, and process each transaction only once.</li>
<li><strong>Reconcile daily.</strong> Match your orders against your M-Pesa statement.</li>
<li><strong>Get the paperwork ready.</strong> Going live needs a registered paybill or till, so check requirements early.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: treat the callback, not the button click, as the source of truth.</blockquote>','Plan my M-Pesa integration','/proposal.html?category=web-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Many people use Excel for years without touching the features that save hours. If you want a short learning list, start here:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tables (Ctrl+T).</strong> They expand automatically and make formulas easier to read.</li>
<li><strong>XLOOKUP or VLOOKUP.</strong> Pull a price, name or status from another list instead of retyping it.</li>
<li><strong>PivotTables.</strong> Summarise thousands of rows by month, branch or product in a few clicks.</li>
<li><strong>Data validation.</strong> Use drop-down lists so everyone enters "Nairobi" the same way, which prevents messy data later.</li>
<li><strong>Conditional formatting.</strong> Highlight overdue invoices or low stock automatically.</li>
</ol>
<p>A good practice exercise: take last month''s sales, turn it into a Table, build a PivotTable by product, and add a simple chart. It takes about thirty minutes and teaches most of the list.</p>
<p>When you outgrow Excel, SQL and Python are the natural next steps, and we mentor teams and individuals through both.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: learn structure first (Tables and clean columns), and the clever formulas become easy.</blockquote>','Ask about training','/book.html?category=training','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Not every enquiry is ready to buy or rent. A short qualification step protects your team''s time and helps serious prospects feel looked after. When someone enquires, ask these five questions, ideally within the first hour:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Budget:</strong> "What range are you comfortable with?"</li>
<li><strong>Timeline:</strong> "When would you like to move or buy?"</li>
<li><strong>Financing:</strong> "Will this be cash, a mortgage or a payment plan?"</li>
<li><strong>Needs:</strong> bedrooms, location, must-haves.</li>
<li><strong>Decision-makers:</strong> "Will anyone else join the viewing?"</li>
</ol>
<p>You can build these questions into your lead form or a WhatsApp quick-reply so the information arrives before you call. Record each lead in a simple sheet or CRM and mark it hot, warm or cold.</p>
<p>Also, reply fast. Prospects usually contact several agents at once, and the first helpful response often gets the viewing.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: a few good questions turn a name and number into a prospect you can act on.</blockquote>','Get a lead-gen plan','/proposal.html?category=digital-marketing','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>An A/B test compares two versions, such as two headlines or two ad images, to see which performs better. It is easy to run and easy to misread. Follow these rules:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Change one thing.</strong> If the button colour and headline both differ, you will not know which mattered.</li>
<li><strong>Pick your success metric first.</strong> For example, form submissions. Choosing it after seeing results invites cherry-picking.</li>
<li><strong>Do not stop at the first good-looking result.</strong> Decide the sample size or duration in advance, and run for at least one or two full weeks so weekday and weekend behaviour are both included.</li>
<li><strong>Allow for chance.</strong> With small numbers, a gap of a few conversions can easily be luck. A statistical test, such as a two-proportion test, tells you how surprising the difference really is.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the result is unclear, that is still information: the change probably does not matter much, so test something bigger.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: decide the rules before you look at the numbers.</blockquote>','Plan a test with us','/book.html?category=data-analytics','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Most database problems are design problems that were easy to avoid on day one. Keep these habits:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Give every table a primary key</strong>, such as an ID column that never changes. Do not use phone numbers or names as identifiers, since they change.</li>
<li><strong>Store each fact once.</strong> Put customers in a customers table and refer to them from orders using a foreign key, rather than retyping their details on every order.</li>
<li><strong>One value per column.</strong> Avoid cramming "Nairobi, Mombasa" into a single cell. Use a related table instead.</li>
<li><strong>Use consistent names</strong>, for example lowercase with underscores, and singular or plural throughout.</li>
<li><strong>Choose proper data types.</strong> Dates as dates and amounts as numbers, not text.</li>
<li><strong>Add constraints</strong> (required fields, unique emails) so bad data is rejected at the door.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before building, sketch the tables and relationships on paper. Ten minutes of drawing saves days of rework.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: clean structure up front is far cheaper than cleaning data later.</blockquote>','See database services','/our-services.html','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>"Going digital" can feel overwhelming. A one-page roadmap makes it manageable. Draw three columns:</p>
<h3>Now (0 to 3 months)</h3>
<p>Quick, low-risk wins: a working website, a Google Business Profile, tracking enquiries in one sheet, and a backup routine.</p>
<h3>Next (3 to 9 months)</h3>
<p>Improvements that need a little build time: online payments, an automated follow-up email, a simple dashboard, or a customer database.</p>
<h3>Later (9+ months)</h3>
<p>Bigger bets: a custom system, a mobile app, or integrating your tools.</p>
<p>To place items, score each idea on <strong>effort</strong> and <strong>value</strong>. High value and low effort goes in Now. Revisit the page every quarter and move cards across.</p>
<p>Keep each card tied to a business outcome, for example "reduce time spent on invoicing", not just a technology name.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: a roadmap is a priority list with timing, not a technology shopping list.</blockquote>','Book a roadmap session','/book.html?category=consulting','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Automation does not have to mean a big project. These five are often realistic within days:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Form to sheet.</strong> Send website or Google Form enquiries straight into a spreadsheet or CRM.</li>
<li><strong>Instant lead alert.</strong> Notify the right person by email or messaging app the moment an enquiry arrives.</li>
<li><strong>Invoice and payment reminders</strong> sent on a schedule instead of by memory.</li>
<li><strong>Scheduled reports.</strong> Email yourself a weekly summary of sales or leads automatically.</li>
<li><strong>Welcome and follow-up emails</strong> triggered when someone signs up or books.</li>
</ol>
<p>Tools such as Zapier, Make, Google Apps Script or small custom scripts can connect many of these. Choose the simplest option that works, and check what each tool costs as volume grows.</p>
<p>Before automating a process, write it down step by step. If it is unclear to a person, it will be unclear to software.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: fix the process on paper, then automate it.</blockquote>','Tell us what to automate','/proposal.html?category=software-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>The first few emails after someone signs up get unusually good attention, so make them count. A reliable three-part structure:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Email 1 (immediately): deliver and set expectations.</strong> Thank them, give what you promised, and say how often you will write.</li>
<li><strong>Email 2 (day 2 or 3): be useful.</strong> Share one practical tip or short guide that solves a common problem.</li>
<li><strong>Email 3 (day 5 to 7): invite a next step.</strong> Show a result or story (with permission), and offer one clear action such as booking a call.</li>
</ol>
<p>Some good habits:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use one call to action per email.</li>
<li>Write in a personal tone, with the sender being a real person.</li>
<li>Segment by interest, so property investors do not receive tips meant for online shops.</li>
<li>Always include an easy unsubscribe link. It keeps your list healthy and is good practice.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: give value first, ask second.</blockquote>','Set up email automation','/book.html?category=digital-marketing','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>A homepage has one job: help the right visitor understand your offer and take a next step. Check that yours includes:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>A clear headline</strong> that says what you do and for whom, without jargon.</li>
<li><strong>A primary button</strong> above the fold, such as "Get a quote" or "Book a visit".</li>
<li><strong>Your main services</strong>, in short blocks that link to detail pages.</li>
<li><strong>Proof.</strong> Real testimonials with permission, logos of genuine clients, or photos of your actual work.</li>
<li><strong>Contact options</strong> that suit local habits: phone, WhatsApp and email, with your Nairobi location if customers visit you.</li>
<li><strong>Trust signals</strong> such as secure HTTPS, a clear privacy policy and business registration details where relevant.</li>
</ol>
<p>Then look at the page on your phone. If you must pinch and zoom, or the button is hidden, fix that before anything else.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: say what you do, prove it, and make the next step obvious.</blockquote>','Plan a better website','/proposal.html?category=web-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Many software projects go over budget because people imagined different things. A one-page specification, a light version of a formal SRS, keeps everyone aligned. Include:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Goal.</strong> The problem in one or two sentences, and how you will know it is solved.</li>
<li><strong>Users and roles.</strong> Who logs in, and what each person can do.</li>
<li><strong>Core features.</strong> A short list of must-haves, written as "A manager can approve a leave request".</li>
<li><strong>Out of scope.</strong> What you are deliberately not building now.</li>
<li><strong>Data and integrations.</strong> What information is stored, and which systems connect, such as M-Pesa or email.</li>
<li><strong>Acceptance criteria.</strong> How each feature will be checked as done.</li>
</ol>
<p>Add a rough sketch of the main screens if you can. Even hand-drawn pages remove a surprising amount of guesswork.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: writing down what is out of scope is as valuable as listing what is in.</blockquote>','Get help with a spec','/proposal.html?category=software-design','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>A survey is only as good as its questions. Before you send anything, work through this checklist:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Start with the decision.</strong> Write down what you will do with each answer. If nothing, drop the question.</li>
<li><strong>One idea per question.</strong> "Was the staff friendly and fast?" mixes two things.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid leading wording.</strong> "How great was our service?" nudges people. Ask "How would you rate our service?"</li>
<li><strong>Use consistent scales</strong> such as 1 to 5 with labelled ends, and offer "Not applicable" when needed.</li>
<li><strong>Keep it short.</strong> Respect people''s time and completion rates tend to rise.</li>
<li><strong>Pilot with five people</strong> to catch confusing questions.</li>
</ol>
<p>For field work, mobile tools such as ODK and KoBoToolbox let enumerators collect data offline and sync later, with validation rules that reduce typing errors.</p>
<p>Also think about who will answer. If only your happiest customers respond, the results will look better than reality.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: pilot first, since a bad question multiplied by a thousand responses is expensive.</blockquote>','Design a survey with us','/book.html?category=data-analytics','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Our free sessions work best when we can get straight to your real situation. If you plan to book, jot down these points beforehand:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Your goal.</strong> What would success look like in three to six months? More enquiries, less admin, better reports?</li>
<li><strong>Where you are today.</strong> Existing website, tools, spreadsheets or ad accounts, even if they are messy.</li>
<li><strong>A budget range.</strong> A rough range in KES helps us suggest realistic options rather than an ideal-world plan.</li>
<li><strong>Timeline.</strong> Is there a launch date, season or deadline driving this?</li>
<li><strong>Examples you like</strong>, such as websites, apps or campaigns, and what you like about them.</li>
<li><strong>Who decides.</strong> If someone else must approve, consider inviting them.</li>
</ol>
<p>Do not worry if you cannot answer everything. Part of the session is helping you figure out the questions.</p>
<p>You can choose the service area when booking so we come prepared with the right person and ideas.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: bring your goal, your current setup and a rough budget.</blockquote>','Book your session','/book.html?category=consulting','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>You do not need to be technical to catch the most common problems. Set a timer for 30 minutes and check:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Ownership.</strong> Is your domain registered in your name or company, and do you hold the login? Who controls your hosting, social accounts and ad accounts?</li>
<li><strong>Access.</strong> Turn on two-factor authentication on email, domain and hosting. Remove accounts of former staff or freelancers.</li>
<li><strong>Updates.</strong> Are your website platform, plugins and themes up to date?</li>
<li><strong>Backups.</strong> Do they exist, and when did anyone last test one?</li>
<li><strong>Tracking.</strong> Is analytics installed and recording enquiries correctly?</li>
<li><strong>Speed and security.</strong> Does the site load over HTTPS, and does it pass a basic PageSpeed test on mobile?</li>
</ol>
<p>Write down every "I do not know" answer. Those are your priority list, and often the quickest fixes are simply giving yourself control.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: if you do not control your accounts, you do not control your business presence.</blockquote>','Request a technical audit','/proposal.html?category=consulting','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>The cheapest way to learn what works on Facebook and Instagram is a structured, small test. Here is a simple plan:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>One objective.</strong> Choose leads, messages or sales. Do not mix goals in one campaign.</li>
<li><strong>Three audiences.</strong> For example: a location plus interest group, people who engaged with your page, and a lookalike or broad audience.</li>
<li><strong>Three creatives.</strong> Test different angles, such as a benefit, a customer problem and an offer, rather than tiny colour changes.</li>
<li><strong>Equal daily budgets</strong> and at least five to seven days, so each variation gets a fair run.</li>
<li><strong>Judge by cost per lead or sale</strong>, not likes.</li>
</ol>
<p>Then pause the weakest options and move budget to the winner, adding one new creative to challenge it. Keep the offer clear and make sure your landing page or WhatsApp reply loads fast and matches the ad.</p>
<p>Always follow the platform''s current advertising policies, which change from time to time.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: test angles, not details, and judge by cost per result.</blockquote>','Plan my ad campaign','/book.html?category=digital-marketing','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Databases rarely fail suddenly. They slow down gradually until someone complains. Watch for these signs:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pages or reports that were fast now take many seconds</strong>, especially as data grows.</li>
<li><strong>Timeouts at busy hours</strong>, such as month end or during a promotion.</li>
<li><strong>Queries scanning whole tables</strong> to find a few rows.</li>
<li><strong>Disk or memory usage creeping up</strong> without a clear reason.</li>
<li><strong>Users blocking each other</strong> because long operations hold locks.</li>
</ol>
<p>Common, well-known fixes include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Adding <strong>indexes</strong> on columns used for searching, joining and sorting. Do not index everything, because indexes slow down writes.</li>
<li>Reading only the columns you need rather than <em>SELECT *</em>.</li>
<li>Using your database''s query-plan tool (such as EXPLAIN) to see what is really happening.</li>
<li>Archiving very old data and running routine maintenance.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: measure first. Find the slowest queries before changing anything.</blockquote>','Ask for a performance review','/book.html?category=database-management','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Fixing a bug after customers find it costs more than catching it during development, in money, time and trust. Three practices make a big difference:</p>
<h3>1. Automated tests for critical paths</h3>
<p>You do not need to test everything. Start with the flows that earn or lose money: sign-up, checkout, payment confirmation and report totals. Write tests that run automatically on each change.</p>
<h3>2. Code review</h3>
<p>A second pair of eyes catches mistakes, spreads knowledge and keeps the code readable. Even a quick review of every change helps.</p>
<h3>3. A staging environment</h3>
<p>Try changes on a copy of the system before they reach real users, and make sure you can roll back quickly.</p>
<p>When commissioning software, ask the developer how it will be tested and how releases are done. Vague answers are a warning sign.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: protect the few flows your business cannot afford to break.</blockquote>','Discuss QA for your project','/proposal.html?category=software-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Great content cannot rank if search engines struggle to access your site. Work through this checklist:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>HTTPS everywhere.</strong> Redirect the http version to https.</li>
<li><strong>Mobile-friendly layout</strong> and reasonable load speed.</li>
<li><strong>One clear title and description per page</strong>, each unique and describing that page.</li>
<li><strong>One H1 per page</strong>, with logical H2 and H3 headings below.</li>
<li><strong>Readable URLs</strong> such as /services/web-design rather than /page?id=47.</li>
<li><strong>A sitemap</strong> submitted in Google Search Console, plus a check for crawl errors.</li>
<li><strong>Descriptive image names and alt text.</strong></li>
<li><strong>No broken links.</strong> Redirect old URLs to new ones after a redesign.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you serve a local audience, claim and complete your Google Business Profile as well.</p>
<p>Search Console is free and shows which queries bring people to you and which pages have problems. Check it monthly.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: make it easy for search engines first, then work on content.</blockquote>','Get a website review','/book.html?category=web-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Dashboards turn raw data into decisions, but the tool matters less than the questions. Here is a practical comparison:</p>
<h3>Looker Studio</h3>
<ul>
<li>Free to use and quick to start.</li>
<li>Connects easily to Google Sheets, Google Analytics and Google Ads.</li>
<li>Good for marketing and light business reporting.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Power BI</h3>
<ul>
<li>Stronger data modelling and calculations for larger or more complex data.</li>
<li>Fits well if you already use Excel and Microsoft tools.</li>
<li>Sharing and some features need licences, so check current pricing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Whichever you choose, follow these rules:</p>
<ol>
<li>Start by writing the five questions the dashboard must answer.</li>
<li>Show the most important numbers at the top.</li>
<li>Use clear labels and consistent time periods.</li>
<li>Make sure the underlying data is clean and refreshed on a schedule.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: a dashboard is only as good as the data and questions behind it.</blockquote>','Request a dashboard','/proposal.html?category=data-analytics','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Changing a design in Figma takes minutes. Changing the same thing after it has been coded can take days. That is why we recommend prototyping before development.</p>
<h3>A simple process</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Map the journey.</strong> List the steps a user takes to complete the main task.</li>
<li><strong>Sketch rough screens.</strong> Pen and paper is fine.</li>
<li><strong>Build a clickable prototype.</strong> Link the screens so people can tap through.</li>
<li><strong>Test with five users.</strong> Give them a task, such as "Book a viewing", and watch without helping. Note where they hesitate.</li>
<li><strong>Revise and repeat.</strong> Small rounds are enough.</li>
</ol>
<p>Even a handful of testers usually reveals the biggest usability problems. The point is to learn, not to impress.</p>
<p>Once approved, the prototype doubles as a clear brief for developers, which reduces misunderstandings and rework.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: find the confusing parts on screen, not in production.</blockquote>','Start a design project','/book.html?category=software-design','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Buying off-plan means paying for something that is not finished, so trust is your main selling point. Make these things visible in your marketing:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Who you are.</strong> Developer name, past completed projects and a real office address.</li>
<li><strong>Progress.</strong> Regular dated site photos or short video updates.</li>
<li><strong>Clear terms.</strong> Price, payment plan, what is included and expected handover timeline.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation.</strong> Explain that buyers should verify title and approvals, and encourage them to consult their own lawyer.</li>
<li><strong>Easy visits.</strong> Offer scheduled site tours and a virtual walkthrough for diaspora buyers.</li>
</ol>
<p>Avoid inflated promises about returns. Honest, specific information performs better over time and protects your reputation.</p>
<p>For ads, aim at a distinct audience (for example, location-based and diaspora groups), send people to a landing page with a short enquiry form, and follow up quickly.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: with off-plan, proof and transparency do the selling.</blockquote>','Plan your project launch','/proposal.html?category=digital-marketing','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Growing businesses often get stuck on how to staff technical work. Each route has a place:</p>
<h3>Hire in-house when</h3>
<ul>
<li>The work is continuous and central to your product.</li>
<li>You can afford salary, equipment and management time.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Outsource to an agency when</h3>
<ul>
<li>The work is project-based or needs several skills (design, development, data) for a short period.</li>
<li>You want a defined price and timeline.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Use freelancers when</h3>
<ul>
<li>The task is small and well defined.</li>
<li>You have someone able to brief and review the work.</li>
</ul>
<p>Whichever you choose, protect yourself: keep ownership of code, domains and accounts, require documentation, and ensure that knowledge does not live only in one person''s head.</p>
<p>Many businesses use a hybrid: an outside team to design and build, then a part-time or in-house person to run it.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: choose based on how continuous the work is, not on the cheapest hourly rate.</blockquote>','Discuss your options','/book.html?category=consulting','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Spreadsheets are great until several people edit them, files multiply and nobody trusts the totals. When it is time to move to a proper database, follow this path:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Inventory.</strong> List every sheet, who uses it and what decisions rely on it.</li>
<li><strong>Clean first.</strong> Fix duplicates, inconsistent spellings, mixed date formats and missing values.</li>
<li><strong>Design the structure.</strong> Split data into tables (customers, orders, products) with clear keys.</li>
<li><strong>Do a trial import.</strong> Load a copy and compare counts and totals with the originals.</li>
<li><strong>Run in parallel</strong> for a short period so staff can compare and trust the new system.</li>
<li><strong>Cut over and archive.</strong> Make the old files read-only and keep a backup.</li>
</ol>
<p>Train the people who will use it, and provide simple forms or screens so they are not typing into raw tables.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: reconciling totals between old and new is the proof that nothing was lost.</blockquote>','Plan a data migration','/proposal.html?category=database-management','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>In Kenya and much of East Africa, Android phones are widely reported to be the majority of smartphones, so many businesses begin there. But an app is not always the first step. Consider:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A mobile-friendly website or progressive web app (PWA).</strong> No store download, faster to launch and often cheaper. Good for catalogues, bookings and forms.</li>
<li><strong>A native Android app.</strong> Best when you need device features, notifications, strong offline use or a smooth experience.</li>
<li><strong>Cross-platform frameworks</strong> such as Flutter or React Native. One codebase for Android and iOS, useful if both audiences matter.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Questions to ask first</h3>
<ol>
<li>What can users do in the app that they cannot on the website?</li>
<li>Will they need it offline or on low-data connections?</li>
<li>How will you get people to install and keep it?</li>
</ol>
<p>Design for small screens, modest storage and limited data from day one.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: validate the idea with the simplest version before building the full app.</blockquote>','Talk about your app idea','/book.html?category=software-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Before you announce your shop, test it as a customer would. Check each of these:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Product pages.</strong> Clear photos, honest descriptions, price in KES, size or variant options and stock status.</li>
<li><strong>Delivery.</strong> Show fees and expected times by area, for example Nairobi versus upcountry, before checkout.</li>
<li><strong>Payments.</strong> Offer M-Pesa and card options your customers use, and place a test order for each.</li>
<li><strong>Policies.</strong> Returns, refunds, privacy and contact details that are easy to find.</li>
<li><strong>Order notifications.</strong> Confirmation messages to the customer and an alert to you.</li>
<li><strong>Speed and mobile.</strong> Browse and buy on a real phone.</li>
<li><strong>Analytics.</strong> Track visits, add-to-cart and completed orders so you can improve.</li>
</ol>
<p>Finally, decide who handles stock updates and customer questions each day. A shop that responds slowly loses trust quickly.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: place a real test order, pay with M-Pesa, and follow it through to delivery.</blockquote>','Plan my online shop','/proposal.html?category=web-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>People often get stuck choosing between Python and R. The honest answer is that either can take you far. A few guidelines:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Choose R</strong> if your work is statistics-heavy, such as research, surveys, health or M&amp;E, and you like built-in statistical tools and strong charting.</li>
<li><strong>Choose Python</strong> if you also want automation, web scraping, machine learning or to connect data work to software and apps.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before either, learn <strong>SQL</strong>. Most business data lives in databases, and a few weeks of SQL (SELECT, WHERE, JOIN, GROUP BY) will make you useful quickly.</p>
<h3>A starter path</h3>
<ol>
<li>Excel with clean tables and PivotTables.</li>
<li>Basic SQL.</li>
<li>One language, Python or R, for cleaning, summarising and plotting data.</li>
<li>A small real project from your own work.</li>
</ol>
<p>Learning sticks when you use your own data, so pick a question you actually care about.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: finish one real project in one language before exploring the other.</blockquote>','Ask about mentorship','/book.html?category=training','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Good SEO content starts with the reader''s question, not your keyword list. Try this process:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Collect real questions.</strong> Use the questions customers ask you, Google''s "People also ask" boxes and autocomplete suggestions.</li>
<li><strong>Understand intent.</strong> Is the person trying to learn, compare or buy? Match the page type to the intent.</li>
<li><strong>Answer early.</strong> Give the direct answer in the opening lines, then explain.</li>
<li><strong>Structure clearly.</strong> Use descriptive headings, short paragraphs, lists and examples from your own experience.</li>
<li><strong>Add local relevance.</strong> Mention Nairobi, Kenyan prices, regulations or M-Pesa where it genuinely applies.</li>
<li><strong>Link internally.</strong> Point to related articles and to the page where readers can take action.</li>
</ol>
<p>Update older posts when information changes, since fresh and accurate pages build trust over time. SEO is gradual, so aim for steady publishing over many months rather than a burst.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: one thorough answer to a real question beats five thin posts.</blockquote>','Read our blog','/blog.html','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Wrong conclusions often come from unclean data rather than bad maths. Use this checklist before any analysis:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Keep the raw file untouched.</strong> Work on a copy, and record every change you make.</li>
<li><strong>Remove duplicates.</strong> Decide what makes a record unique, such as ID or phone plus date.</li>
<li><strong>Standardise formats.</strong> One date format, one phone format, trimmed spaces and consistent capitalisation.</li>
<li><strong>Unify categories.</strong> "Nbi", "Nairobi" and "nairobi " should become one value.</li>
<li><strong>Handle missing values deliberately.</strong> Leave, fill or exclude them, and note which you chose and why.</li>
<li><strong>Check for outliers and impossible values.</strong> A negative age or a sale of KES 5 million in a shop that sells airtime deserves a look. Investigate before deleting.</li>
</ol>
<p>Tools such as Excel Power Query, SQL, Python (pandas) and R (tidyverse) can repeat these steps automatically, so next month''s data is cleaned the same way.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: write your cleaning steps down or script them, so the results are repeatable.</blockquote>','Get help with your data','/book.html?category=data-analytics','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Architecture means how the parts of a system fit together. Two common styles:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monolith.</strong> One application containing everything (users, orders, payments). Simple to build, test and deploy.</li>
<li><strong>Microservices.</strong> Many small services that communicate over a network. Flexible for large teams, but they add complexity in deployment, monitoring and debugging.</li>
</ul>
<p>For most startups and SMEs, a well-organised monolith is the practical starting point. You can separate parts later if real needs appear, such as heavy load on one component or independent teams.</p>
<h3>What matters more</h3>
<ol>
<li>Clean boundaries between modules, so pieces can be split later.</li>
<li>A well-designed database.</li>
<li>Security basics: authentication, permissions and input checks.</li>
<li>Logging and monitoring, so you see problems early.</li>
<li>A plan for backups and recovery.</li>
</ol>
<p>Choose technology your team can maintain. A simple design that is understood beats a fashionable one that nobody can fix.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: start simple, keep modules tidy, and scale when evidence demands it.</blockquote>','Review my architecture','/book.html?category=software-design','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Digital transformation sounds huge, but it usually starts with a single frustrating process. A practical approach:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pick one process</strong> that is slow, error-prone or costly, such as quoting, invoicing, stock counting or client onboarding.</li>
<li><strong>Map it.</strong> Draw each step, who does it, how long it takes and where mistakes happen.</li>
<li><strong>Simplify before digitising.</strong> Remove unnecessary steps. Automating a bad process just makes mistakes faster.</li>
<li><strong>Choose the lightest tool</strong> that fits: a shared form, a spreadsheet with validation, a ready-made app, or custom software.</li>
<li><strong>Pilot with a small group</strong> for a few weeks.</li>
<li><strong>Measure.</strong> Compare time taken and errors before and after.</li>
</ol>
<p>Involve the people who do the work. If they help design the change, they are far more likely to use it.</p>
<p>Once one process works, repeat with the next. Small proven steps build confidence and budget for bigger ones.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: prove value on one process, then scale.</blockquote>','Build my roadmap','/proposal.html?category=consulting','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Vague requests produce vague quotes. Spend thirty minutes on a brief and you will save weeks later. Include:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Background.</strong> Who you are, what you do, and the problem you want to solve.</li>
<li><strong>Goals.</strong> Measurable outcomes, for example "cut invoice processing from two days to two hours".</li>
<li><strong>Users.</strong> Who will use it and roughly how many.</li>
<li><strong>Must-have versus nice-to-have features.</strong> Label them honestly.</li>
<li><strong>Integrations.</strong> M-Pesa, accounting software, email or existing databases.</li>
<li><strong>Constraints.</strong> Deadline, budget range, devices, language, and any data-protection needs.</li>
<li><strong>References.</strong> Links to products or screens you like.</li>
</ol>
<p>Do not worry about technical terms. Describe what people need to do and let the team suggest how.</p>
<p>When you are ready, our proposal form walks you through these points, and you can attach any notes or sketches.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: describe the problem and the outcome, and be honest about budget and deadline.</blockquote>','Submit a proposal','/proposal.html','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>If every link you share looks the same in your analytics, you cannot tell which effort worked. UTM parameters fix that by adding labels to your URLs.</p>
<p>A tagged link looks like this: <em>yoursite.co.ke/offer?utm_source=facebook&amp;utm_medium=paid_social&amp;utm_campaign=december_promo</em></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>utm_source:</strong> where it came from (facebook, newsletter, whatsapp).</li>
<li><strong>utm_medium:</strong> the type (paid_social, email, organic).</li>
<li><strong>utm_campaign:</strong> the specific campaign name.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Rules that keep it clean</h3>
<ol>
<li>Use lowercase and underscores, because "Facebook" and "facebook" are counted separately.</li>
<li>Keep a shared sheet of the naming you use.</li>
<li>Never tag internal links on your own site.</li>
<li>Check results in Google Analytics under traffic acquisition, then compare enquiries per source.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: consistent naming turns scattered clicks into clear answers.</blockquote>','Set up my tracking','/book.html?category=digital-marketing','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Websites age quickly if nobody looks after them. Put this routine in your calendar for the first Monday of each month:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Take a backup</strong> and confirm it actually downloaded or stored.</li>
<li><strong>Update</strong> your CMS, plugins and themes, ideally on a staging copy first for important sites.</li>
<li><strong>Test key actions.</strong> Submit your contact form, run a test checkout and click your phone and WhatsApp buttons.</li>
<li><strong>Check speed</strong> with PageSpeed Insights and note any big drop.</li>
<li><strong>Scan for broken links</strong> and fix or redirect them.</li>
<li><strong>Review users.</strong> Remove old accounts and confirm strong passwords with two-factor sign-in.</li>
<li><strong>Look at analytics</strong> for odd traffic spikes or pages that suddenly lose visits.</li>
<li><strong>Renew early.</strong> Check domain, hosting and SSL expiry dates.</li>
</ol>
<p>Keep a simple log of what you did. If something breaks, it tells you what changed.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: an expired domain or a failed form can cost more than a year of maintenance.</blockquote>','Ask about maintenance','/book.html?category=web-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Connecting your system to services like payment gateways, SMS providers or maps saves effort, but external services can be slow, change, or go down. Build defensively:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Keep secrets secret.</strong> Store API keys in environment variables or a secrets manager, never in public code or front-end scripts.</li>
<li><strong>Set timeouts and retries.</strong> Do not let one slow call freeze your whole app. Retry with increasing delays, and stop after a few attempts.</li>
<li><strong>Make operations idempotent.</strong> If the same payment notification arrives twice, process it once.</li>
<li><strong>Verify webhooks.</strong> Check signatures or source details before trusting incoming data.</li>
<li><strong>Log requests and responses</strong> (without sensitive data) so you can investigate disputes.</li>
<li><strong>Read the version notes.</strong> Providers deprecate old endpoints, so follow their announcements.</li>
<li><strong>Show a friendly fallback</strong> when the service is unavailable.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: assume every external call can fail, and decide in advance what happens then.</blockquote>','Plan an integration','/proposal.html?category=software-development','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Forecasting does not need advanced machine learning to be useful. Start simple, then improve only if it earns its keep.</p>
<h3>Step by step</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Plot your data.</strong> Look for trend, seasonality (December peaks, school terms, rainy seasons) and unusual events.</li>
<li><strong>Create baselines.</strong> The naive forecast uses the last value. The seasonal naive uses the same month last year. A moving average smooths noise.</li>
<li><strong>Hold back recent data.</strong> Forecast it without peeking, then compare predictions with what really happened.</li>
<li><strong>Measure the error</strong> with something simple like MAE or MAPE.</li>
<li><strong>Try better methods</strong>, such as exponential smoothing or ARIMA, and keep one only if it clearly beats the baseline.</li>
</ol>
<p>Always present a range rather than a single figure, and update the forecast as new data arrives. Remember that a forecast assumes the future resembles the past, so adjust for known changes like price rises or a new branch.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: a model that cannot beat a simple baseline is not worth the complexity.</blockquote>','Discuss forecasting','/book.html?category=data-analytics','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Customer records are valuable, and Kenya''s Data Protection Act, 2019 expects organisations that handle personal data to protect it. Without turning this into legal advice, these controls are widely recommended:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Least privilege.</strong> Give each person and application only the access they need. Avoid sharing one admin login.</li>
<li><strong>Strong authentication.</strong> Unique passwords, a password manager and two-factor sign-in for admin tools.</li>
<li><strong>Parameterised queries.</strong> They prevent SQL injection, one of the most common web attacks.</li>
<li><strong>Encryption.</strong> Use HTTPS for data in transit and consider encrypting backups and sensitive fields.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the database off the public internet</strong> where possible, behind a firewall.</li>
<li><strong>Patch regularly</strong> and remove default accounts.</li>
<li><strong>Collect less.</strong> Data you never store cannot leak.</li>
<li><strong>Log and review access</strong> to spot unusual activity.</li>
</ol>
<p>For compliance questions, consult a qualified legal adviser.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: limit who can see data, protect how it moves, and store only what you need.</blockquote>','Book a security check','/book.html?category=database-management','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Accessibility is about making your product usable by as many people as possible, including people with low vision, motor difficulties or older phones. Six improvements that usually cost little:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Good contrast.</strong> Aim for the WCAG guideline of at least 4.5:1 for normal text.</li>
<li><strong>Readable sizes.</strong> Use body text around 16px or larger and allow zooming.</li>
<li><strong>Large tap targets.</strong> Buttons should be comfortable to hit with a thumb, with space between them.</li>
<li><strong>Do not rely on colour alone.</strong> Pair red error text with an icon or a clear message.</li>
<li><strong>Label every form field</strong> with visible text, not just placeholder text that disappears.</li>
<li><strong>Add alt text</strong> to meaningful images, and make sure the page can be used with a keyboard.</li>
</ol>
<p>Test by squinting at your screen, turning brightness down, or using a free contrast checker. Accessibility checks in browser developer tools can find many issues automatically.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: accessible design is simply clear design, and it widens your audience.</blockquote>','See our design services','/our-services.html','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>You do not need to read code to hold a project to a professional standard. Ask for:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Access to the code repository</strong> (such as GitHub) in your organisation''s name, with a history of changes.</li>
<li><strong>A README</strong> explaining what the system does, how to set it up and how to deploy it.</li>
<li><strong>A working demo</strong> of each milestone on a test site, not just screenshots.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence of testing.</strong> A list of what was tested, and automated tests for important flows.</li>
<li><strong>A documented release process</strong> with backups and the ability to roll back.</li>
</ol>
<p>Also ask where it is hosted, who holds the logins, and what ongoing costs apply.</p>
<p>If you feel unsure, an independent code or architecture review is a modest cost compared with rebuilding a system that cannot be maintained. A reviewer can check structure, security, performance and how easy it would be for another developer to take over.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: insist on ownership, documentation and demos.</blockquote>','Request a code review','/book.html?category=consulting','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Long reports with dozens of charts go unread. Aim for one page with this layout:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Goal for the month.</strong> For example, 60 qualified enquiries.</li>
<li><strong>Five headline numbers.</strong> Spend, enquiries, cost per enquiry, enquiry-to-sale rate and revenue, each compared with last month.</li>
<li><strong>What worked.</strong> The best channel, ad or post, with one line on why you think so.</li>
<li><strong>What did not.</strong> Be honest, because this is where learning happens.</li>
<li><strong>Next actions.</strong> Three specific changes for next month, with an owner for each.</li>
</ol>
<p>Use plain language and add short explanations beside figures. A chart is worth including only if it changes a decision.</p>
<p>Automate the data collection with a dashboard or scheduled export, so that your time goes into thinking rather than copying numbers.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: every number on the page should lead to a decision.</blockquote>','Get monthly reporting','/proposal.html?category=digital-marketing','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Monitoring and evaluation (M&amp;E) helps projects show what they are achieving. Good M&amp;E begins with good indicators. For each one, define:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>What exactly is counted.</strong> "Number of women aged 18 to 35 who completed the training" is clearer than "empowered women".</li>
<li><strong>Baseline.</strong> Where things stand before you start.</li>
<li><strong>Target and timeframe.</strong> What you aim for, by when.</li>
<li><strong>Data source and method.</strong> Registers, surveys, phone interviews, or administrative records.</li>
<li><strong>Frequency and owner.</strong> Who collects it and how often.</li>
<li><strong>Disaggregation.</strong> By gender, age or county, if relevant.</li>
</ol>
<p>Keep the number of indicators small. Fewer, well-collected measures beat a long list nobody can update.</p>
<p>Digital tools help. Mobile collection with ODK or KoBoToolbox, a clean database, and a live dashboard let teams see progress without waiting for end-of-year reports.</p>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: if you cannot say how an indicator will be collected, it is not ready yet.</blockquote>','Build an M&E system','/book.html?category=data-analytics','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>No platform is best for everyone. Compare your options on three questions: who will update it, what must it do, and how will it grow?</p>
<h3>Website builders</h3>
<p>Fast and affordable for simple brochure sites. Less flexible, and you may be tied to the provider.</p>
<h3>WordPress and similar CMS platforms</h3>
<p>Flexible, with a large ecosystem for blogs, business sites and shops. They need regular updates and care with plugins.</p>
<h3>Custom builds</h3>
<p>Best for unusual workflows, portals, complex integrations or high performance needs. They cost more up front but fit your process exactly.</p>
<p>Questions to ask:</p>
<ol>
<li>Do I need to edit content myself?</li>
<li>Are there special features such as M-Pesa, booking or member logins?</li>
<li>What are the ongoing costs for hosting, licences and updates?</li>
<li>Can I export my content if I leave?</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>Key takeaway: choose the simplest platform that meets today''s needs and leaves room to grow.</blockquote>','Ask us a question','/contact.html','ACTIVE',NOW(),NOW());
<p>Over the past months we have shared tips on marketing, websites, software, design, databases, data and consulting. We hope some of them saved you time or money.</p>
<p>Here is where to go from here:</p>
<ul>
