## Start with the listing page

Before spending on ads, make sure the page you send people to is ready. A weak page wastes every shilling you put behind it.

- **Clear price and location.** State the price in KES and name the estate or neighbourhood, for example Kilimani, Syokimau or Ruaka. Vague locations lose serious buyers.
- **Photos taken in daylight.** Show every room, the compound, the access road and the parking. Photos of the gate and the street help buyers judge the real location.
- **Key facts up top.** Bedrooms, bathrooms, size, service charge, water and backup power arrangements.
- **One obvious action.** A call button and a WhatsApp button that open directly from a phone.

## Choose channels by buyer type

Different properties attract different people, so match the channel to the audience.

1. **Rentals and starter homes:** Facebook, Instagram and WhatsApp status work well because renters browse casually and share with friends.
2. **Mid-range family homes:** Google search ads and Facebook lead forms catch people who are actively looking.
3. **Land and investment plots:** Diaspora buyers often start on Facebook and YouTube, then want a video call. Plan for time-zone differences when you set follow-up times.

## Show, do not just tell

A short phone video walking through the home often answers more questions than ten photos. Keep it under a minute, steady, and shot in good light. Add a caption with the price and location so it works even with the sound off.

For plots, include a map pin, distance to the main road, and the state of the title documents you can legitimately share. Never imply that approvals or titles exist if you cannot show them. Trust is the main asset of an agent, and one misleading post can undo it.

## Capture leads properly

Many agents lose leads because enquiries sit in a personal phone. Set up a simple system:

- Use one enquiry form or WhatsApp number per campaign so you know which advert produced the lead.
- Record each lead with name, phone, budget and the property they asked about.
- Reply the same day. Speed matters more than a perfect message.

## Follow up with a rhythm

Most buyers do not decide on the first call. A simple pattern works well: reply within hours, send extra photos or a video the next day, offer a viewing slot within the week, and check back after a viewing. Keep notes so you never ask the same question twice.

## Measure what you can

Track how many enquiries each channel brought, how many became viewings, and how many closed. You do not need complicated software for this. A shared sheet with consistent columns is enough to start. After a month you will see which channel deserves more budget and which should be paused.

A caution on numbers: results vary a great deal by area, price band and season, so avoid copying someone else''s benchmarks. Use your own data from the first few campaigns as the baseline.

## A sample weekly routine

- Monday: publish new listings with fresh photos.
- Wednesday: post a short video tour of one property.
- Friday: review enquiries, update availability and remove sold properties promptly.

Removing sold listings is a small habit that protects your reputation. Nothing frustrates a buyer more than falling for a home that is already gone.

