## Why a business account helps

A business account separates customer chats from personal ones and adds features designed for selling.

- **Business profile.** Add your name, description, hours, address and website so customers know you are real.
- **Catalogue.** List products or services with photos, prices and short descriptions.
- **Quick replies.** Save answers to common questions such as delivery areas or payment options.
- **Labels.** Tag chats as new enquiry, awaiting payment or delivered.
- **Away and greeting messages.** Let people know when you will reply.

## Set it up properly

1. Use a dedicated number if you can, not only a personal one, so staff changes do not lose your history.
2. Write a profile description in plain words: what you sell and where you deliver.
3. Add your best ten products or services to the catalogue first. You can expand later.
4. Write quick replies for price, delivery, payment and returns. Shortcuts like /price save time every day.

## Make payment simple

Tell customers exactly how to pay. If you accept M-Pesa, give the till or paybill number clearly and say what to include as the reference. Confirm receipt promptly with a short message. Customers feel safe when they see a clear process.

## Bring people into the chat

Your chat is only useful if people find it. Add a WhatsApp button to your website, Facebook page and Instagram bio. Print the QR code or short link on receipts, packaging and shop signage. In adverts, use a click-to-chat link so the conversation begins with one tap.

## Keep the tone human

Automation is helpful, but customers can tell when they are talking to a script. Use quick replies as a starting point and then add the person''s name and a specific detail. A reply such as "Hi Wanjiru, yes the blue one is in stock and we can deliver to Thika tomorrow" works far better than a copied paragraph.

## Respect permission

Do not add people to broadcast lists without consent. Ask first, and make it easy to opt out. Sending unwanted promotions can get your number reported or blocked, and it harms how people see your brand. Share useful updates such as new stock, restocks and seasonal offers rather than daily pushes.

## Track results simply

Use labels to see how many chats become orders. At the end of each week, note how many new enquiries you received, how many replied after your follow-up, and how many bought. If many people ask the same question, add the answer to your catalogue or website so the chat gets shorter.

## Common mistakes

- Leaving chats unanswered overnight with no away message.
- Quoting prices from memory, which leads to disputes later.
- Mixing personal and business contacts on a single number.
- Skipping the profile details, which makes the account look unofficial.

## When to go beyond the app

Once you handle dozens of chats each day, the free app may feel tight. At that point, consider the WhatsApp Business Platform with a shared inbox, which several staff can use together. That usually involves a provider and costs, so weigh it against your volume before committing.

## Next step

Not sure how WhatsApp fits with your website and adverts? [Book a digital marketing session](/book.html?category=digital-marketing) and we will plan a simple chat-to-sale flow for your brand.','Misgina Fitwi',NULL,57,NOW() - INTERVAL 9 DAY,NOW());

## Claim your Google Business Profile

This is the single most useful free step for a local business. Search for your business name and claim the profile, then complete every field.

- Exact business name, as it appears on your signage.
- Address or service area, and a phone number you actually answer.
- Opening hours, including public holiday changes.
- Categories that match what you really do. Choose a main category carefully.
- Photos of your premises, team and work.
- A short description in natural language.

Keep your details identical everywhere online. Small differences in your name or number confuse both people and search engines.

## Build a page for each core service

Many small sites have one page that says "we do everything". Search engines and visitors prefer specific pages. If you offer three services, give each its own page with:

1. A clear title that names the service and the town.
2. A plain description of what is included.
3. Typical process and timing, without making promises you cannot keep.
4. Real photos or examples.
5. A visible way to contact you.

Write for people first. Stuffing a page with repeated keywords reads badly and does not help.

## Ask for reviews the right way

Reviews influence both rankings and trust. After a job is done and the customer is happy, send a short message with a direct link to leave a review. Reply to every review, including critical ones, with a calm and helpful answer. Never buy or write fake reviews. Platforms can remove them and the damage to your reputation is hard to repair.

## Make the site fast and mobile friendly

Most local searches happen on phones, often on mobile data. Check that your pages load quickly, text is readable without zooming, and the phone number can be tapped to call. A slow site loses visitors before they read a word.

## Earn local mentions

Links and mentions from other local sources signal that you are real and active. Practical places to start:

- Local business directories that are well maintained.
- Your supplier or partner websites, where appropriate.
- Community groups and event sponsorships that publish sponsor lists.
- Local news or blog features, if you have a genuine story.

Quality matters more than quantity. A handful of relevant mentions beat hundreds of low-quality ones.

## Be patient and measure

SEO results usually take weeks to months, and no honest agency can promise a ranking position. Track a few signals instead:

- Calls and direction requests from your business profile.
- Visits to your service pages.
- Enquiries that mention they found you on Google.

Google Search Console is free and shows which searches bring people to your site. Review it monthly and improve the pages that nearly rank but do not yet attract clicks.

## A simple monthly checklist

- Add one new photo and one update to your profile.
- Reply to all new reviews.
- Refresh one service page with better detail.
- Check that your hours and contact details are still correct.

Consistent small improvements beat a single big push followed by months of silence.

