## Start by measuring

Do not guess. Run your key pages through a free tool such as Lighthouse in Chrome or PageSpeed Insights. Test the home page and one important inner page, such as a product or booking page. Note the biggest issues rather than chasing a perfect score. Where possible, also test on a real mid-range phone, not only on your laptop.

## Fix images first

Images are usually the heaviest part of a page. Common problems and fixes:

- **Oversized files.** A photo straight from a phone can be several megabytes. Resize to the largest size it is displayed at.
- **Old formats.** Use WebP or AVIF where supported, with a JPEG fallback if needed.
- **Everything loads at once.** Add lazy loading so images below the fold load only when the visitor scrolls.
- **No dimensions.** Set width and height so the layout does not jump while images load.

## Reduce JavaScript

Scripts must be downloaded, parsed and run, and weaker phones take longer to do this. Review what you load:

1. Remove plugins and libraries you no longer use.
2. Avoid loading a whole library for one small feature.
3. Defer non-essential scripts such as chat widgets until after the page is usable.
4. Be selective with third-party trackers. Each one adds weight and delay.

## Use caching and a CDN

Caching tells the browser to keep files it has already downloaded so repeat visits are faster. A content delivery network stores copies of your files closer to visitors. For many small sites, enabling both through your host or a free CDN tier brings a clear improvement. Confirm that updated files still reach visitors, for example by using versioned file names.

## Choose fonts carefully

Custom fonts are another common slowdown. Limit yourself to one or two families and only the weights you need. Use font-display: swap so text appears immediately in a fallback font while the custom one loads. System fonts are a perfectly good, fast choice.

## Keep the server response quick

If the server takes a long time to respond, nothing else helps. Check your hosting plan and location, and consider hosting nearer your audience. For dynamic sites, look at slow database queries and cache the results of expensive operations. Our [database management services](/book.html?category=database-management) can help if queries are the bottleneck.

## Design for small screens and small budgets

- Make buttons large enough to tap with a thumb.
- Keep forms short. Ask only for what you need.
- Avoid auto-playing video and huge background images.
- Offer a lightweight version of heavy content, such as a transcript or a compressed video option.

## Test after every change

Speed work can quietly break things. After each change, check the page visually, test forms and payment steps, and re-run your speed test. Keep a record of before and after results so you know what actually helped.

## Do not chase every score

A page that feels fast to real users matters more than a number in a tool. Focus on the moment the main content appears and the moment the page becomes usable. If those are quick on a modest phone, you are in good shape.

