## Separate vanity metrics from outcome metrics

Impressions, reach and likes show activity. They are not worthless, but they are far from your goal. Outcome metrics connect more directly to the business:

- Enquiries, calls or form submissions.
- Bookings or sales.
- Cost per enquiry and cost per sale.
- Revenue or profit from a campaign.

Start from the outcome you want and work backwards to the numbers that explain it.

## Understand rates and denominators

A rate is a count divided by something. A conversion rate, for example, is the number of people who took action divided by the number who visited. Always ask what the denominator is. One hundred enquiries from ten thousand visitors is a different story from one hundred enquiries from five hundred visitors.

Averages can also hide a lot. If most customers spend a modest amount and a few spend a very large amount, the average looks higher than what a typical customer spends. Look at the median as well, which is the middle value.

## Beware of small samples

Imagine one advert gets 3 enquiries and another gets 5 after a single day. It is tempting to declare a winner, but with so few events, chance alone can easily produce that gap. As a rough guide, wait until each option has a meaningful number of results before you decide, and treat early differences cautiously. The smaller the numbers, the more they bounce around.

## Correlation is not cause

Sales rose after you started a campaign, but several things may have changed at once: the season, a salary week, a competitor''s stock-out or a public holiday. Before crediting one cause, ask what else changed. Where possible, compare against a similar period or a group that did not see the advert.

## Be careful with testing

A/B testing compares two versions of a page or advert. To get a trustworthy answer:

1. Change one main thing at a time.
2. Split the audience randomly.
3. Decide in advance what you are measuring and how long to run the test.
4. Do not stop the moment one version looks ahead.

Running many tests and reporting only the winner increases the chance of fooling yourself. Some differences will appear purely by luck.

## Watch seasonality and timing

Many Kenyan businesses see natural cycles, such as month-end spending, school-term demand or festive periods. Compare like with like, for example this December against last December rather than against November. Plot results over time, and look for patterns before judging a change.

## Track the whole journey

Customers rarely buy after one touch. They might see an advert, visit later through a search, and finally call. If you only credit the last click, you may undervalue early channels. Even a simple question on your enquiry form, such as how did you hear about us, adds useful context.

## Keep your data clean

Use consistent names for campaigns, tag your links, and record offline results such as phone sales. Messy data leads to confident but wrong conclusions.

## A simple monthly review

- Choose three to five outcome metrics and keep them stable.
- Record them in one place, with the same definitions each month.
- Note what you changed and when.
- Ask what the numbers cannot tell you, and where you are guessing.

